  "query_preview": "/FRIDAY THROUGH MONDAY/...\nIssued at 153 PM CST Mon Nov 23 2026\n\nThe potential for some more active weather arrives Saturday as a \ntrough pivots across the northern Plains. There is still some \nuncertainty regarding the strength of this system and resulting \nprecipitation potential. At this time, the latest probabilities show \nthe potential of receiving at least 0.01 of QPF to be near 20 \npercent with the potential of receiving 0.10 of QPF shrinking to only \n10 to 15 percent. With model inconsistency high in regards to any \ndevelopment, confidence remains low in seeing any precipitation. \nDespite this, have included at least a mention of some light rain \nand snow showers for much of western and southwest Nebraska. If \nshowers were to develop, areas across the Panhandle will have the \ngreatest potential to see anything as the higher elevation will help \nto support light showers. A dry forecast will return by Saturday \nnight lasting through Sunday as upper level ridging and surface high \npressure return to the area. \n\nTemperatures will return to near normals in the low to mid 50s for \nthe weekend as cool air advection begins to push southward into \nNebraska over the weekend. With 850 mb temperatures in the -1 to 6 \ndegree range combined with partly to mostly cloudy skies, surface \ntemperatures will hover near normals. Overnight lows, however, will \nremain fairly mild in the mid to upper 20s through the weekend. \nThese lows are still nearly 10 degrees above normals for this time \nof year.",
